var e={id:`saas-steering-isdb-sla`,h:{en:`SaaS steering: ISDB, applications and SLAs for Microsoft 365 and Salesforce-style traffic`,hi:`SaaS steering: Microsoft 365 aur Salesforce jaise traffic ke liye ISDB, applications aur SLAs`},body:{en:`
<p>Once the exit is decided, SD-WAN has to pick the <b>best ISP</b> for each SaaS service and move it when the quality drops. The tools are the ones from the rules and SLA modules. This chapter applies them to SaaS and adds the cloud-specific details: which ISDB objects to use, how the first packet is classified, which probe to use, and what numbers to target.</p>
<h3>How a SaaS flow is identified</h3>
<table class="cmp"><tr><th>Method</th><th>Matches on</th><th>Good for</th><th>Watch out</th></tr><tr><td><b>ISDB entry</b> (<code>internet-service-name</code>)</td><td>Destination IP, port and protocol from Fortinet's database</td><td>Steering from the very first packet</td><td>Only as good as the database; services behind a shared CDN address may be missed</td></tr><tr><td><b>Application</b> (application control signature)</td><td>Deep inspection of the traffic</td><td>Services with many addresses, apps inside HTTPS</td><td>Needs a few packets; the first packet may take the default route</td></tr><tr><td><b>FQDN address</b></td><td>The IP learned from DNS for a name</td><td>Small, stable name lists</td><td>Depends on the FortiGate's own DNS answers matching the client's</td></tr></table>
<p>For the first packet, the FortiGate looks up the destination IP, port and protocol (the "3-tuple") in the ISDB and in the application control cache. A Fortinet technical article explains that rules with an ISDB entry are checked first for traffic identified by ISDB. If the same IP is already cached as an application by application control (for example Microsoft Teams), the traffic skips the ISDB rules and is matched against rules that use applications or that have no ISDB at all. So two rules, one by ISDB and one by application, can behave differently for the same server. The article also shows how to force an address into an ISDB category with <code>firewall internet-service-extension</code>, to stop a cache entry from changing the match.</p>
<h3>Which ISDB objects for Microsoft 365</h3>
<p>The ISDB has a base object <b>Microsoft-Office365</b> and refined subsets such as <b>Microsoft-Office365.Published</b> and <b>Microsoft-Office365.Published.Allow</b>. Fortinet community answers show that engineers ask which subset corresponds to Microsoft's <i>Optimize</i>, <i>Allow</i> and <i>Default</i> categories, and that the answer is in the Fortinet article on the ISDB groups. A practical approach: start with the base object for the rule, check the object list in the GUI of your release (use the search box on the Internet Service selection page), and use a subset only when you must separate classes. Look up the Salesforce entries the same way: search the list for "Salesforce" and select the objects that match the products you use.</p>
<pre class="code"><span class="c">Reference configuration (not run in the simulator)</span>
<span class="c">! BR1-FGT: SaaS steering. wan1 = member 1 (ISP1), wan2 = member 2 (ISP2)</span>
config system sdwan
    config service
        edit 30
            set name "M365-LOCAL"
            set mode sla
            set internet-service enable
            set internet-service-name "Microsoft-Office365"
            config sla
                edit "Default_Office_365"
                    set id 1
                next
            end
            set priority-members 1 2
        next
    end
end</pre>
<p>The GUI path is <b>Network, SD-WAN, SD-WAN Rules, Create New</b>. In the destination section choose <b>Internet Service</b> instead of an address, and pick the objects. For the strategy choose <b>Lowest Cost (SLA)</b> and select the Performance SLA entry and the members. If you want the first member to stay preferred while it meets the SLA, put it first in the list.</p>
<h3>The probe: the built-in Default_Office_365 and its trap</h3>
<p>FortiOS includes predefined health checks for well-known SaaS (the SLA module covers them). The one for Microsoft 365 is named <code>Default_Office_365</code> and probes <b>www.office.com</b>. A Fortinet troubleshooting article reports that Microsoft changed the behaviour of that service and restricted HTTP on a related name, so the default probe failed, routes were marked inactive and failover misbehaved. The documented fixes were to remove the affected interface from that health check or to turn off the option that updates the static route, and to use a protocol that the server answers, such as ping. The command that shows the state is <code>diagnose sys sdwan health-check status</code>. The lesson is general: <b>a SaaS probe depends on the SaaS provider, so verify it after every provider change and prefer a probe that you control or that is documented</b>.</p>
<h3>Targets for Teams and other SaaS</h3>
<table class="cmp"><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th><th>Source in the module</th></tr><tr><td>Latency to the Microsoft front door</td><td>under 100 ms</td><td>Microsoft 365 connectivity test tool</td></tr><tr><td>Jitter</td><td>under 30 ms</td><td>same</td></tr><tr><td>Packet loss</td><td>under 1 percent (the Teams quality dashboard classes a stream as poor above 0.1 percent loss, so aim lower in practice)</td><td>Microsoft Teams guidance</td></tr><tr><td>Teams media ports</td><td>UDP 3478 to 3481, in the Optimize category</td><td>Microsoft Teams guidance</td></tr><tr><td>Teams audio DSCP</td><td>EF (46) when QoS markers are enabled</td><td>Microsoft Teams guidance</td></tr></table>
<p>Teams bandwidth per endpoint, in kbit/s up and down: audio 58 recommended (10 minimum), one-to-one video 1,500 recommended (150 minimum), meeting video 2,500 up and 4,000 down recommended, screen sharing 1,500 to 2,500 recommended. Microsoft states that Teams can deliver HD video in under 1.5 Mbit/s and prefers audio over video when bandwidth is short. A branch of 40 simultaneous Teams video participants at the recommended meeting rate therefore needs about 40 x 2.5 = 100 Mbit/s upstream in the worst case. Real concurrency is lower, but this is the order of magnitude to check against your ISP link and your shaping design.</p>
<p>For the SLA of the rule, a starting set for interactive SaaS and media is latency 100 ms, jitter 30 ms and loss 1 percent, tightened for voice by the shaping module's targets. Use the link-cost and hold-down options from the SLA module so that the rule does not flap between ISPs.</p>
<h3>What not to do with Microsoft 365 traffic</h3>
<ul><li>Do not break TLS with deep inspection on the Optimize and Allow domains.</li><li>Do not block QUIC or force UDP to fall back to TCP. Microsoft lists both as known problems.</li><li>Do not change the UDP source NAT mapping in the middle of a session: the Teams guidance asks for NAT session persistence and a properly sized NAT pool.</li><li>Do not rely on a static copy of the endpoints. The ISDB is updated through FortiGuard, so keep the subscription active; Microsoft also publishes its own endpoint web service.</li></ul>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Common mistake.</b> Using only an application match for the SaaS rule and seeing that the first seconds of each session leave over the default route. Either use the ISDB entry too, or accept that the first packet is unclassified and design the default path to be acceptable.</p></div>
<div class="exam"><p><b>Exam trap.</b> The first packet is matched by the destination 3-tuple in the ISDB and the application cache. ISDB rules are checked first for ISDB traffic, but a cached application can send the flow to the application rules. Know the command <code>diagnose sys sdwan health-check status</code> and that a failed default SaaS probe can mark a member dead.</p></div>
<div class="scn"><h4>Microsoft 365 leaves ISP1 even though ISP1 is slower</h4><p>BR1's rule M365-LOCAL prefers ISP2 in the list. Users report slow SharePoint, and the session list shows ISP1. A Teams server address was cached as an application, so the session skipped the ISDB rule and matched an older rule by application that preferred ISP1. The fix was to align the two rules and to add the Teams addresses to the ISDB rule with an internet service extension.</p><p><b>Lesson:</b> Check which rule matched, not which rule you meant.</p></div>
<div class="iv"><p class="q">"How do you steer Microsoft 365 over the best of two ISPs?"</p><p>I create an SD-WAN rule with the Microsoft-Office365 ISDB object as destination, mode Lowest Cost (SLA) with the Default_Office_365 SLA or my own probe, and both ISPs as priority members. I set the thresholds from Microsoft's targets, 100 ms latency, 30 ms jitter and 1 percent loss, avoid deep inspection for those domains, and check the first-packet behaviour and the health check after any change.</p></div>
<div class="kt"><p><b>Key takeaways</b></p><ul><li>ISDB steers from the first packet; applications need a few packets; both can disagree.</li><li>The Microsoft 365 objects are Microsoft-Office365 and its subsets; check the list in your release.</li><li>Default_Office_365 probes www.office.com; verify it, because provider changes can break it.</li><li>Targets: latency under 100 ms, jitter under 30 ms, loss under 1 percent.</li><li>No TLS inspection, QUIC blocking or UDP downgrade for Microsoft 365 traffic.</li></ul></div>
`,hi:`
<p>Exit tay hone ke baad SD-WAN ko har SaaS service ke liye <b>best ISP</b> chunna hai aur quality girne par use hataana hai. Tools wahi hain jo rules aur SLA modules mein hain. Yeh chapter unhe SaaS par laagu karta hai aur cloud-specific details jodta hai: kaun se ISDB objects use karein, pehla packet kaise classify hota hai, kaun sa probe use karein, aur kaun se numbers target karein.</p>
<h3>SaaS flow ki pehchaan kaise hoti hai</h3>
<table class="cmp"><tr><th>Tareeka</th><th>Kis par match</th><th>Kis ke liye achha</th><th>Dhyan rakho</th></tr><tr><td><b>ISDB entry</b> (<code>internet-service-name</code>)</td><td>Fortinet ke database se destination IP, port aur protocol</td><td>Bilkul pehle packet se steering</td><td>Database jitna hi achha; shared CDN address ke peeche ki services chhoot sakti hain</td></tr><tr><td><b>Application</b> (application control signature)</td><td>Traffic ka deep inspection</td><td>Bahut addresses wali services, HTTPS ke andar apps</td><td>Kuch packets chahiye; pehla packet default route le sakta hai</td></tr><tr><td><b>FQDN address</b></td><td>Naam ke liye DNS se mila IP</td><td>Chhoti, stable name lists</td><td>FortiGate ke apne DNS answers ka client ke answers se milna zaroori</td></tr></table>
<p>Pehle packet ke liye FortiGate destination IP, port aur protocol (the "3-tuple") ko ISDB aur application control cache mein dekhta hai. Ek Fortinet technical article samjhata hai ki ISDB se pehchane traffic ke liye ISDB entry wale rules pehle check hote hain. Agar wahi IP application control ne pehle se application (jaise Microsoft Teams) ke roop mein cache kiya hai, to traffic ISDB rules skip karke un rules se match hota hai jo applications use karte hain ya jinme ISDB hai hi nahi. Isliye ek ISDB se aur ek application se bane do rules ek hi server ke liye alag behave kar sakte hain. Article <code>firewall internet-service-extension</code> se kisi address ko ISDB category mein force karna bhi dikhata hai, taaki cache entry match na badle.</p>
<h3>Microsoft 365 ke liye kaun se ISDB objects</h3>
<p>ISDB mein ek base object <b>Microsoft-Office365</b> aur <b>Microsoft-Office365.Published</b> aur <b>Microsoft-Office365.Published.Allow</b> jaise refined subsets hain. Fortinet community ke jawab dikhate hain ki engineers poochhte hain ki kaun sa subset Microsoft ki <i>Optimize</i>, <i>Allow</i> aur <i>Default</i> categories se milta hai, aur jawab Fortinet ke ISDB groups wale article mein hai. Practical tareeka: rule ke liye base object se shuru karo, apne release ke GUI mein object list dekho (Internet Service selection page ka search box use karo), aur subset tabhi use karo jab classes alag karni hon. Salesforce entries bhi isi tarah dhoondho: list mein "Salesforce" search karo aur woh objects chuno jo aapke products se milte hain.</p>
<pre class="code"><span class="c">Reference configuration (not run in the simulator)</span>
<span class="c">! BR1-FGT: SaaS steering. wan1 = member 1 (ISP1), wan2 = member 2 (ISP2)</span>
config system sdwan
    config service
        edit 30
            set name "M365-LOCAL"
            set mode sla
            set internet-service enable
            set internet-service-name "Microsoft-Office365"
            config sla
                edit "Default_Office_365"
                    set id 1
                next
            end
            set priority-members 1 2
        next
    end
end</pre>
<p>GUI path <b>Network, SD-WAN, SD-WAN Rules, Create New</b> hai. Destination section mein address ki jagah <b>Internet Service</b> chuno aur objects chuno. Strategy ke liye <b>Lowest Cost (SLA)</b> chuno aur Performance SLA entry aur members select karo. Agar pehla member SLA poora karte hue preferred rakhna ho to use list mein pehle rakho.</p>
<h3>Probe: built-in Default_Office_365 aur uska trap</h3>
<p>FortiOS mein jaani-maani SaaS ke liye predefined health checks hain (SLA module unhe cover karta hai). Microsoft 365 wala <code>Default_Office_365</code> kehlata hai aur <b>www.office.com</b> ko probe karta hai. Ek Fortinet troubleshooting article batata hai ki Microsoft ne us service ka behaviour badla aur ek related naam par HTTP restrict kiya, isliye default probe fail hua, routes inactive mark hue aur failover galat chala. Documented fixes: us health check se affected interface hataana ya static route update karne wala option band karna, aur aisa protocol use karna jise server answer kare, jaise ping. State dikhane wala command <code>diagnose sys sdwan health-check status</code> hai. Seekh general hai: <b>SaaS probe SaaS provider par nirbhar hai, isliye har provider badlav ke baad use verify karo aur aisa probe prefer karo jo aapke control mein ho ya documented ho</b>.</p>
<h3>Teams aur doosre SaaS ke targets</h3>
<table class="cmp"><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th><th>Source</th></tr><tr><td>Microsoft front door tak latency</td><td>100 ms se kam</td><td>Microsoft 365 connectivity test tool</td></tr><tr><td>Jitter</td><td>30 ms se kam</td><td>wahi</td></tr><tr><td>Packet loss</td><td>1 percent se kam (Teams quality dashboard 0.1 percent se zyaada loss par stream ko poor kehta hai, isliye practice mein kam rakho)</td><td>Microsoft Teams guidance</td></tr><tr><td>Teams media ports</td><td>UDP 3478 se 3481, Optimize category mein</td><td>Microsoft Teams guidance</td></tr><tr><td>Teams audio DSCP</td><td>QoS markers enabled hon to EF (46)</td><td>Microsoft Teams guidance</td></tr></table>
<p>Teams bandwidth per endpoint, kbit/s up aur down: audio 58 recommended (10 minimum), one-to-one video 1,500 recommended (150 minimum), meeting video 2,500 up aur 4,000 down recommended, screen sharing 1,500 se 2,500 recommended. Microsoft kehta hai ki Teams HD video 1.5 Mbit/s se kam mein de sakta hai aur bandwidth kam ho to audio ko video par tarjeeh deta hai. 40 ek saath Teams video participants wali branch ko recommended meeting rate par worst case mein lagbhag 40 x 2.5 = 100 Mbit/s upstream chahiye. Asli concurrency kam hoti hai, par yeh woh order of magnitude hai jise apni ISP link aur shaping design se milao.</p>
<p>Rule ke SLA ke liye interactive SaaS aur media ka starting set latency 100 ms, jitter 30 ms aur loss 1 percent hai, voice ke liye shaping module ke targets se tight karo. SLA module ke link-cost aur hold-down options use karo taaki rule ISPs ke beech flap na kare.</p>
<h3>Microsoft 365 traffic ke saath kya na karein</h3>
<ul><li>Optimize aur Allow domains par deep inspection se TLS mat todo.</li><li>QUIC block mat karo aur UDP ko TCP par fall back mat karwao. Microsoft dono ko known problems batata hai.</li><li>Session ke beech UDP source NAT mapping mat badlo: Teams guidance NAT session persistence aur sahi size ka NAT pool maangti hai.</li><li>Endpoints ki static copy par nirbhar mat raho. ISDB FortiGuard se update hota hai, isliye subscription active rakho; Microsoft apni endpoint web service bhi publish karta hai.</li></ul>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Common galti.</b> SaaS rule ke liye sirf application match use karna aur dekhna ki har session ke pehle kuch seconds default route se jaate hain. Ya to ISDB entry bhi use karo, ya maan lo ki pehla packet unclassified hai aur default path ko acceptable banao.</p></div>
<div class="exam"><p><b>Exam trap.</b> Pehla packet ISDB aur application cache mein destination 3-tuple se match hota hai. ISDB traffic ke liye ISDB rules pehle check hote hain, par cached application flow ko application rules ki taraf bhej sakta hai. Command <code>diagnose sys sdwan health-check status</code> yaad rakho aur yeh bhi ki fail hua default SaaS probe member ko dead mark kar sakta hai.</p></div>
<div class="scn"><h4>Microsoft 365 ISP1 se nikalta hai jabki ISP1 dheemi hai</h4><p>BR1 ka rule M365-LOCAL list mein ISP2 ko prefer karta hai. Users SharePoint ko slow batate hain, aur session list ISP1 dikhati hai. Ek Teams server address application ke roop mein cache tha, isliye session ne ISDB rule skip kiya aur ek purane application rule se match hua jo ISP1 prefer karta tha. Fix: dono rules ko align karna aur Teams addresses ko internet service extension ke saath ISDB rule mein jodna.</p><p><b>Seekh:</b> Dekho kaun sa rule match hua, woh nahi jo aapne socha.</p></div>
<div class="iv"><p class="q">"Do ISPs mein se best par Microsoft 365 kaise steer karoge?"</p><p>Main destination mein Microsoft-Office365 ISDB object wala SD-WAN rule banata hoon, mode Lowest Cost (SLA) Default_Office_365 SLA ya apne probe ke saath, aur dono ISPs priority members. Main thresholds Microsoft ke targets se rakhta hoon, 100 ms latency, 30 ms jitter aur 1 percent loss, un domains ke liye deep inspection se bachta hoon, aur kisi bhi badlav ke baad first-packet behaviour aur health check dekhta hoon.</p></div>
<div class="kt"><p><b>Key takeaways</b></p><ul><li>ISDB pehle packet se steer karta hai; applications ko kuch packets chahiye; dono asahmat ho sakte hain.</li><li>Microsoft 365 objects Microsoft-Office365 aur uske subsets hain; apne release ki list dekho.</li><li>Default_Office_365 www.office.com probe karta hai; use verify karo, kyunki provider badlav use tod sakte hain.</li><li>Targets: latency 100 ms se kam, jitter 30 ms se kam, loss 1 percent se kam.</li><li>Microsoft 365 traffic ke liye TLS inspection, QUIC blocking ya UDP downgrade nahi.</li></ul></div>
`}};export{e as default};